.TH SPINCLASS-SWEATFILE 5 "" "spinclass" "Spinclass Reference"
.SH NAME
spinclass-sweatfile \- sweatfile configuration format
.SH DESCRIPTION
Spinclass sessions are configured via TOML files called
.IR sweatfiles .
These control git excludes, Claude Code permissions, direnv setup,
session entrypoints, lifecycle hooks, the dynamic system-prompt fragment,
MCP server registration, custom start-* plugins, and remote session hosts.
.PP
Sweatfiles are loaded as a hierarchy that merges
.I global
.RI ( ~/.config/spinclass/sweatfile )
\(-> intermediate parent directories \(-> repository-level
.RI ( <repo>/sweatfile )
\(-> worktree-level
.RI ( <worktree>/sweatfile ).
Later definitions override earlier ones according to per-field merge
semantics described below.
.PP
Intermediate parent directories are walked along
.B two
chains: the lexical ancestors of the working directory, and the
ancestors of its symlink-resolved (\fIrealpath\fR) form. Both chains
are inspected in root-first order, bounded above by
.B $HOME
(exclusive \(em loaded as the global file) when the chain runs through
home, or by the filesystem root
.RB ( / ,
exclusive) otherwise. Candidate directories are deduplicated by
canonical (symlink-resolved) path: if a directory in one chain
resolves to the same canonical path as one already loaded, the second
entry is recorded with a skip reason and not re-merged.
.PP
A given physical worktree therefore produces the same merged
configuration whether entered through a canonical path or through a
symlink, provided sweatfiles exist along both chains. When a
filesystem-only-reachable directory has a sweatfile that no path-only
chain can reach (e.g.\& entering through the canonical path skips a
sweatfile that would only be visible via a symlinked alias), the
.B sc validate
output makes this asymmetry explicit by listing every directory it
considered alongside the load/skip status.
.SH TABLES
.SS [claude]
Controls Claude Code session behaviour.
.TP
.B allow
Array of Claude Code permission rules (e.g.\&
.BR Read ", " Edit ", " "Bash(git *)" ", " "mcp__server__tool" ).
.B Merge:
nil = inherit, empty array = clear, non-empty = append.
.SS [git]
.TP
.B excludes
Array of patterns appended to
.IR .git/info/exclude .
.B Merge:
nil = inherit, empty = clear, non-empty = append.
.SS [direnv]
.TP
.B envrc
Array of direnv directive lines written to
.IR .envrc .
When no sweatfile in the cascade sets
.BR envrc ,
it defaults to
.B source_up
(plus
.B use flake
if a
.I flake.nix
exists).
Note that as soon as
.B any
sweatfile sets
.BR envrc ,
those defaults are dropped entirely \(em the generated
.I .envrc
contains only the directives you supply. Include
.B source_up
explicitly if you still want it (e.g.\&
.BR "envrc = [\(dqsource_up\(dq, \(dquse flake . --impure\(dq]" ).
.B Merge:
nil = inherit, empty = clear, non-empty = append (lines accumulate
across the cascade, like the other array fields).
.TP
.B dotenv
Table of key-value pairs written to
.IR .spinclass/env
at session create.
Values support
.B $VAR
expansion (from the create-time environment); the special variable
.B $WORKTREE
expands to the worktree path.
.PP
Declare it as a sub-table:
.PP
.RS
.EX
[direnv.dotenv]
ETSYWEB_PWD = "$WORKTREE"
.EE
.RE
.PP
When
.B dotenv
is non-empty, spinclass appends a
.B dotenv .spinclass/env
directive to the generated
.I .envrc
so direnv actually loads the file into the shell \(em you do
.B not
add that line yourself. This append happens whether
.I .envrc
came from the
.B envrc
default or from an explicit
.B envrc
array; the
.B dotenv
line is added after your directives.
.PP
The inline-table spelling
.RB ( "dotenv = { FOO = \(dqbar\(dq }" )
is equivalent and equally accepted; use whichever reads better.
.B Merge:
map merge (child keys override parent keys).
.SS [sysprompt]
Configures the dynamic system-prompt fragment spinclass contributes to a
clown-launched session (see FDR 0021). The fragment carries a
.B Design records
section: an index of the repository's design records \(em
.IR docs/features ,
.IR docs/adrs ,
and
.I docs/rfcs
by default \(em listed by number, title, and lifecycle
.B status
(read from each file's YAML frontmatter) and grouped by status.
.TP
.B doc-index-dirs
Array of directories (relative to the worktree/checkout root) scanned for
.I NNNN-*.md
design records. Records are genre-tagged by directory
.RB ( docs/features " \(-> " FDR ,
.B docs/adrs
\(->
.BR ADR ,
.B docs/rfcs
\(->
.BR RFC ;
any other directory uses its basename). Scan-if-exists: an absent directory
contributes nothing, so a repository with none of the scanned directories
renders no
.B Design records
section at all. A structurally broken record (unreadable, or a frontmatter
block opened with
.B ---
but never closed) is reported in a trailing diagnostic block instead of
being silently dropped \(em the fragment renders best-effort and never
fails on a malformed doc.
.B Merge:
override, not append \(em unlike the other array fields. A non-empty value
replaces the inherited list (override-wins down the cascade); an explicit
empty array
.RB ( "doc-index-dirs = []" )
disables the index; nil (unset) inherits, falling back to the built-in
default directories when unset everywhere.
.SS [hooks]
Lifecycle hooks executed as shell scripts.
.PP
The
.BR create ", " pre-merge ", " repair ", " on-attach ", and " on-detach
hooks are invoked via
.B sh \-c
with the working directory set to the worktree path.
The environment variable
.B WORKTREE
is set to the absolute worktree path.
The
.B stop
hook follows a different invocation path (run by Claude Code's
.B Stop
event handler with cwd set to the session's working directory) and does
not export
.BR WORKTREE .
.TP
.B create
Script run after worktree creation (before session attach).
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.IP
Spinclass writes and
.B direnv allow
s the generated
.I .envrc
before running this hook, then re-runs a bare
.B direnv allow
after it succeeds. A create hook that mutates
.I .envrc
(for example, appending a directive) therefore does
.B not
need to re-authorize the file itself \(em the final content is
re-allowed automatically.
.TP
.B stop
Script run when the Claude Code session ends (via Stop hook).
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B pre-merge
Shell script run by
.B sc merge
after the worktree branch has been rebased onto the default branch but
before the fast-forward merge into it.
If the script exits non-zero, the merge is aborted and the worktree is
left in its rebased state so the user can fix the issue and retry.
Typical use: run tests or linters as a pre-merge gate.
.IP
By default the hook runs in a transient, detached
.B build worktree
checked out at the exact (post-rebase) commit being merged \(em a hidden
.B .merge-*
sibling under
.BR .worktrees/ .
This frees the session worktree for concurrent edits the moment the
synchronous prefix (rebase, plus any
.B repair
phase) lands \(em especially with the async merge tool, whose prefix runs
before the job id is returned, so the worktree is yours again the moment you
hold the job id. It also makes the gate verify the committed tree rather than
any uncommitted working-tree state. A merge therefore never locks your
worktree: edits made while the hook runs are left for the next merge, since
the gate builds and fast-forwards the sha it pinned when it started.
The build worktree is removed when the hook finishes.
Because the branch stays checked out in the session worktree, the build
worktree is in
.B detached HEAD
state: a hook that reads the current branch name (e.g.
.BR "git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD" )
sees
.B HEAD
rather than the branch.
Set
.B disable-merge-build-worktree
(below) to run the hook in place in the session worktree instead.
When this hook is set, the spinclass PreToolUse hook auto-approves the
.B mcp__plugin_spinclass_spinclass__merge-this-session
and
.B mcp__plugin_spinclass_spinclass__check-this-session
tools (whichever is registered \(em see
.B disable-merge
below) so Claude Code does not prompt: the pre-merge hook acts as the
gate instead.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B post-merge
Shell script run by
.B sc merge
.B after
the merge has landed on the default branch \(em the mirror image of
.BR pre-merge .
Typical use: trigger a deploy, notify a service, or record the landing
somewhere, keyed on the commit that actually shipped.
.IP
It runs only on a
.B fully successful
merge: the fast-forward completed and, when git sync is on, the push to the
remote succeeded. Any earlier failure (gate, landing rebase, teardown, push)
returns before it. The hook is
.B non-fatal:
by the time it runs the merge cannot be undone, so a non-zero exit does
.B not
fail the merge \(em it emits a
.B severity=warn
test point carrying the hook's output, and the merge still reports success.
Re-running the merge would find nothing to merge, so a failed post-merge hook
is something to act on out of band, not to retry.
.IP
Crucially, post-merge runs
.B after the per-repo merge lock is released
(see
.B disable-merge-queue
below and
.B FDR 0022
in the source tree). A deploy trigger measured in minutes would otherwise
queue every other session in the repo behind it; the lock protects
gate + land + push, all of which are complete by then. The merge tool call
does still wait for the hook before returning.
.IP
The hook runs in the session worktree when it still exists (teardown may have
removed it) and otherwise in the main checkout \(em which is sitting on the
merged tip either way. There is no detached build worktree: the merge has
already landed, so there is nothing to isolate. Unlike
.BR pre-merge ,
.B inactivity-timeout
does not apply; a hook that can wedge should bound itself (e.g.
.BR "timeout 300 deploy.sh" ).
Beyond the usual environment (including
.BR WORKTREE ),
the hook receives:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B SPINCLASS_MERGED_SHA
\(em the commit that landed. On a queued landing that had to be rebased past a
moved default branch this is the
.B landing
sha, not the originally pinned one.
.IP \(bu 2
.B SPINCLASS_MERGED_BRANCH
\(em the branch that was merged.
.IP \(bu 2
.B SPINCLASS_DEFAULT_BRANCH
\(em the branch it landed on.
.IP \(bu 2
.B SPINCLASS_MERGE_PUSHED
\(em
.BR 1 " when the landing was pushed to the remote, " 0
when it is local-only (e.g.
.BR "sc run --local-only" ).
A deploy trigger should gate on this.
.IP \(bu 2
.B SPINCLASS_REPO_PATH
\(em the main checkout.
.RE
.IP
Applies to worktree sessions and to implicit (main-checkout) sessions, whose
push is unconditional \(em there
.B SPINCLASS_MERGED_BRANCH
and
.B SPINCLASS_DEFAULT_BRANCH
are the same ref.
.B sc check
and
.B check-this-session
never run it: nothing lands.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B disable-post-merge
Boolean. Suppresses an inherited
.B post-merge
command without clearing the string (mirrors
.BR disable-repair ).
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B repair
Shell script run by
.B sc merge
as a
.B REPAIR
phase \(em after the rebase and the nothing-to-merge check, but before the
.B pre-merge
verify hook \(em to auto-fold mechanical fixes (formatting, lint-repair) into
the commit being merged. The canonical value is
.BR "conformist --commit --amend --exit-zero-on-fix" .
.IP
Unlike
.BR pre-merge ,
repair runs
.B in the session worktree
(not the detached build worktree): it amends the post-rebase
.B HEAD
in place, which moves the branch ref and leaves the working tree clean
together. Running it in the build worktree would discard the amend, because
the merge fast-forwards the originally pinned sha (see
.B FDR 0018
in the source tree). spinclass detects whether the command amended anything by
comparing
.B HEAD
before and after \(em the command is expected to exit 0 whether or not it
changed the tree (hence
.BR --exit-zero-on-fix );
a non-zero exit aborts the merge. The pin then reads the post-repair
.BR HEAD ,
so the verify hook and fast-forward merge act on the repaired commit.
.IP
Repair is
.B merge-only
\(em
.B sc check
and
.B check-this-session
do not run it. It applies to worktree sessions only; implicit (main-checkout)
sessions are out of scope because their
.B HEAD
may already be pushed, which
.B "conformist --amend"
refuses. Because the amend re-signs
.BR HEAD ,
a locked commit-signing agent fails repair (and thus the merge) rather than
producing an unsigned commit. A repair that amends before a later verify
failure leaves the amended commit on the session branch.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B pre-commit
Shell command installed as a per-session git
.B pre-commit
hook at
.BR "sc start" / "sc resume"
time, repairing staged content at
.B authoring time
so each commit is conformant in history (rather than folding fixes into the
top commit at merge, as
.B repair
does). The canonical value is
.BR "conformist --staged --exit-zero-on-fix" ,
which formats only the staged blobs and restages them \(em no extra commit, no
amend. The hook is installed into
.B <worktree>/.spinclass/hooks/pre-commit
and scoped to that worktree alone via
.B extensions.worktreeConfig
plus a per-worktree
.BR core.hooksPath ,
so it never leaks into the parent checkout or sibling worktrees. It is
.B best-effort and non-blocking:
a missing formatter (failed
.BR "command -v" )
or any non-zero exit never blocks the commit.
.IP
Because git consults exactly one hooks directory, the installed
.B <worktree>/.spinclass/hooks
is a
.B composing dispatcher:
it captures the repo's pre-existing hooks dir (the prior
.B core.hooksPath
or
.BR $GIT_COMMON_DIR/hooks )
and, for every native hook present there, delegates to it after running the
formatter on
.BR pre-commit ,
preserving the native hook's arguments, stdin, and exit code. So a repo's own
.BR pre-commit / commit-msg / pre-push /...
hooks keep firing rather than being shadowed; a blocking native hook still
blocks the commit. Note the captured original is usually the
.B shared
.BR $GIT_COMMON_DIR/hooks ,
so a blocking native hook there gates every worktree's commits, not one. A
nonzero formatter exit (e.g. a stale formatter rejecting its own flags) is
surfaced loudly with its stderr rather than silently swallowed, though the
commit still proceeds. Worktree sessions only; implicit (main-checkout) sessions
are out of scope (spinclass does not install hooks in a user's main checkout).
See
.B docs/plans/2026-06-17-precommit-hook-composition-design.md
in the source tree.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B disable-pre-commit
Boolean. Suppresses an inherited
.B pre-commit
command (mirrors
.BR disable-repair ).
On an already-installed worktree this is a true
.B uninstall:
spinclass unsets its per-worktree
.B core.hooksPath
and removes its dispatcher, so the repo's native hooks fire normally again. This
is also the rollback for the composing hook.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B pre-merge-output-format
String. One of
.BR raw " (default) or " tap-ndjson .
Controls how spinclass interprets the
.B pre-merge
hook's stdout when assembling the
.BR merge-this-session " / " check-this-session
response. When set to
.BR tap-ndjson ,
spinclass parses the hook's stdout as a TAP-14 ndjson stream via
.B tap/go/pkgs/{reader,ndjson}
and, on failure, emits each failing test record as a structured
.B failure:
field in the YAMLish diagnostic instead of the trailing-N-lines
.BR tail: .
The madder blob referenced by
.B resource_link:
holds the parsed ndjson rather than the raw stdout, and the MCP
.B ResourceLinkContent
block's
.B mimeType
is set to
.B application/x-ndjson
(versus
.B text/plain
when
.BR format = raw ).
On parse failure (the hook didn't actually emit TAP, or emitted zero
parseable records), the response falls back to a 15-line
.B tail:
of raw stdout so degenerate output is still visible.
The
.B tail:
field remains the default visibility signal when
.BR format = raw .
.B sc validate
rejects any value outside the closed set
.BR { raw,\ tap-ndjson } .
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B on-attach
Script run when a session attaches to the worktree, fired by
.B sc start
or
.B sc resume
after the session state file has been written but before the
configured session entrypoint
.RI ( "[session].start " "or " "[session].resume" )
is exec'd.
Typical use: warm caches, kick off background helpers (e.g.\& a tmux
side-pane, a watcher process), or emit a session-start log line.
A failing
.B on-attach
script is logged as a warning but never blocks session start \(em it
must not gate attach.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B on-detach
Script run when the session detaches from the worktree, fired by
.B sc start
or
.B sc resume
after the entrypoint exits and the session state has been updated
to
.BR running-detached " or " inactive
(per the
.B [session].liveness-probe
result).
Typical use: tear down on-attach side processes, post a session-end
notification, or rotate logs.
A failing
.B on-detach
script is logged as a warning; spinclass continues with cleanup
either way.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B disallow-main-worktree
Boolean.
When true,
.B sc resume
refuses to attach to the main worktree.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B tool-use-log
Boolean.
When true, a PostToolUse hook logs tool invocations.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B disable-merge
Boolean.
When true,
.B sc merge
exits non-zero before any git operation runs, and the
.B merge-this-session
MCP tool is replaced in the tool catalog by
.B check-this-session
\(em the two tools are mutually exclusive, gated on this flag.
.B check-this-session
runs the configured
.B [hooks].pre-merge
command without merging, providing an agent-CI surface for repositories
where merges must go through external review (e.g., protected branches
that require pull requests). The
.B sc check
CLI subcommand is available regardless of this flag.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B disable-merge-queue
Boolean.
When true, disables the per-repo merge serialization lock (issue #235);
merges then fail immediately if the default branch moved during the
pre-merge gate instead of queuing, rebasing, and re-gating under the
lock.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B disable-repair
Boolean.
When true, suppresses an inherited
.B [hooks].repair
command without having to clear the string \(em the
.B REPAIR
phase is skipped and the merge proceeds straight to the
.B pre-merge
verify hook.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B disable-nix-gc
Boolean.
When true,
.B sc close
and
.B sc clean
skip the post-removal worktree-scoped Nix garbage collection.
By default (or when this flag is unset), spinclass enumerates the gc
roots whose link path resolves into the worktree being removed,
expands each root's closure, and attempts
.B nix-store \-\-delete
on each path; nix's own liveness check refuses paths still kept alive
by other roots, so the blast radius is bounded to "what only this
worktree was holding."
The
.B sc close \-\-nix\-gc=<true|false>
flag overrides this setting per-invocation.
The collection is a silent no-op when
.B nix-store
is not on
.BR PATH ,
or when the worktree has no gc roots.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B disable-merge-build-worktree
Boolean.
When true, the
.B pre-merge
hook runs in place in the session worktree (the legacy behavior) instead of
in a detached build worktree.
By default (or when this flag is unset) the hook runs in a transient
.B .merge-*
worktree pinned to the committed sha being merged \(em see
.B pre-merge
above for the rationale and the detached-HEAD caveat.
Set this when a hook genuinely needs the session worktree's path or its
checked-out branch ref, accepting that the session worktree is then frozen
for the hook's duration and that uncommitted state is what gets verified.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B disable-worktree-path-rewrite
Boolean.
When false (the default), the PreToolUse hook rewrites a tool-call path that
targets the
.I parent checkout
(\fI<repo>/X\fR) into the active worktree (\fI<repo>/.worktrees/<name>/X\fR),
covering the file-reading and -writing tools (\fBRead\fR, \fBWrite\fR,
\fBEdit\fR, \fBNotebookEdit\fR, \fBGlob\fR, \fBGrep\fR, \fBBash\fR) and the
moxy/MCP path tools (\fBfolio_*\fR, \fBrg_search\fR, \fBget-hubbed copy-*\fR,
and the like). The corrected call proceeds without a prompt; the rewrite is
surfaced in a system message. Paths already inside the worktree, under
.B .worktrees/
or
.B .spinclass/
, or outside the parent checkout entirely (\fB$HOME\fR, \fB/tmp\fR, sibling
repos, \fB/nix/store\fR) pass through untouched. The rewrite is a no-op for
implicit (main-checkout) sessions, which have no separate worktree.
When true, the rewrite is disabled; the opt-in
.B disallow-main-worktree
deny (if also set) then applies instead.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B disable-implicit-sessions
Boolean.
When true, an agent started in a repository's main checkout is
.I not
materialized as a spinclass session on
.BR SessionStart :
the hook handler is a no-op, leaving the main checkout without a session
identity (the pre-feature behavior).
By default (or when this flag is unset) a Claude Code agent attached to a
repo's main checkout \(em a git repository on its default branch, not an
.B sc start
worktree \(em becomes a first-class implicit session, visible in
.B sc list
(marked
.BR main ),
addressable in cross-session chat, and mergeable via
.B merge-this-session
(hook-then-push, no rebase).
This is the rollback lever for the implicit-sessions feature.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B inactivity-timeout
String (a Go duration such as
.BR \(dq180s\(dq " or " \(dq3m\(dq ).
When set, an activity watchdog wraps the
.B pre-merge
hook: every line the hook writes to stdout/stderr bumps a last-activity
timestamp, and if the hook produces no output for longer than this
duration its subprocess is killed (via context cancellation of the
.B sh \-c
process) and the merge/check fails with a distinct
.B inactivity-timeout
error rather than the generic non-zero-exit message.
This auto-kills a wedged hook instead of letting it run until the outer
MCP/clown request deadline, and applies to both the synchronous
.RB ( merge\-this\-session " / " check\-this\-session )
and asynchronous
.RB ( *\-this\-session\-async )
hook paths.
An inactivity kill is distinct from a user cancel
.RB ( session\-job\-cancel ):
only the former yields the inactivity error.
Unset (the default), empty, or a non-positive value disables the
watchdog \(em the hook runs without an inactivity bound, as before.
Choose a duration comfortably longer than the hook's quietest legitimate
phase (e.g. a long silent compile or download step) to avoid false
kills.
An unparseable or negative value is a
.B sc validate
error.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B auto-rebuild-on-resume
Boolean.
When true,
.B sc resume
re-applies a worktree's setup (as
.BR "sc rebuild" " does)"
before attaching whenever its recorded setup fingerprint is stale \(em the
merged sweatfile config, the spinclass binary, or the build-time-pinned tools
changed since the setup was last applied.
Default (false): a stale worktree only prints a warning on resume; refresh it
explicitly with
.BR "sc rebuild" .
External-tool version drift (e.g. a changed formatter binary behind an
unchanged hook command) is not detected, since the config is unchanged.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.SS [session\-entry]
Controls what command is exec'd when attaching to a session, how
detached worker sessions are launched, what environment is injected,
how liveness is detected after detach, and how long tombstones are
retained.
.TP
.B start
Argv array for new sessions.
Defaults to
.BR $SHELL .
Each element supports
.B $VAR
expansion against the session environment, so
.B \[dq]$SPINCLASS_SESSION_ID\[dq]
substitutes at exec time.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B resume
Argv array for resumed sessions.
When nil, falls back to
.BR start .
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B spawn\-entry
Argv array for the detached-harness a spawned worker session boots
into, exec'd
.B directly
by
.B sc spawn
and detached
.B sc fork \-\-brief
(FDR-0017 Piece 1: spinclass no longer wraps the worker in a
multiplexer \(em the harness self-detaches and returns promptly, e.g.
clown's
.BR \-\-clown\-attach=spawn ).
.B {prompt}
is replaced by the driver's brief verbatim, staying within its single
argv element \(em no shell joining or re-splitting \(em and
.B {dir}
by the worker worktree's absolute path.
Defaults to
.BR "[\(dqclown\(dq, \(dq\-\-clown\-attach=spawn\(dq, \(dq\-\-\(dq, \(dq{prompt}\(dq]" ;
override per the sweatfile cascade for a different harness.
Template contract: the argv must detach the session and return
promptly \(em
.B sc spawn
blocks until the worker's hello chat message arrives (60s default
deadline), so a harness that blocks in the foreground burns the
deadline.
The exec'd process inherits the session env including any keys from
.BR [session\-entry].env ,
carrying the
.I worker's
.B SPINCLASS_*
identity \(em never the driver's.
.B sc validate
warns when no element contains
.B {prompt}
(legal, but the worker would boot without the driver's brief).
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B spawn\-window
Argv template exec'd fire-and-forget right after the
.B spawn\-entry
launches (before the hello wait, so the harness boot is watchable
live): it opens a terminal window onto the freshly spawned worker.
.B {id}
expands to the worker's session key and
.B {dir}
to the worker worktree's absolute path; this is a leaf argv \(em
.BR {entry} " and " {prompt}
are rejected by
.BR "sc validate" ,
which also warns when no element contains
.BR {id} " or " {dir}
(the window command could not identify its session).
The command runs with the worker's
.B SPINCLASS_*
identity and cwd set to the worker worktree; start and exit failures
are logged warnings, never spawn failures.
No default \(em opening windows is a desktop preference.
A typical value points at a small user script that dispatches per
platform, e.g.\&
.BR "[\(dqsc-spawn-window\(dq, \(dq{id}\(dq, \(dq{dir}\(dq]"
wrapping
.I kitty \-\-detach \-\-title sc/$1 \-\-directory $2 sc resume $1
on Linux or
.I open \-na kitty \-\-args ...
on macOS.
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B model\-flags
Map from a clown provider name
.RI ( "or profile name, see below" )
to the CLI flag that provider's binary uses to select a model.
The lookup key is resolved by scanning the resolved
.B spawn\-entry
for, in order,
.RB ( \-\-provider " or " \-\-provider= )
then
.RB ( \-\-profile " or " \-\-profile= ),
defaulting to
.B claude
(clown's own default) when none are present.
An explicit
.B \-\-provider
always wins over
.BR \-\-profile ,
matching clown's own precedence; when only
.B \-\-profile
is present, spinclass cannot resolve which provider it implies (that
requires querying clown's own profile registry), so the profile name
itself is used as the lookup key \(em a
.B model\-flags
entry may be keyed by profile name instead of provider name for this
case.
Consulted when the
.RB ( spawn\-session " / " fork\-session )
.B model
param, or the equivalent
.RB ( "sc spawn \-\-model" " / " "sc fork \-\-brief \-\-model" )
CLI flag, is set: the flag and the requested model
.RI ( alias )
are spliced into
.B spawn\-entry
immediately after its literal
.B \(dq\-\-\(dq
provider-args separator.
The requested value is validated against a fixed alias set
.RB ( sonnet ", " opus ", " haiku ", or " fable )
.B only
when the resolved key is
.BR claude ;
for any other key (juggler, a profile name, ...) the value is passed
through unvalidated \(em spinclass has no registry to check it
against, so a bad value only surfaces as a failure from the provider
itself.
Defaults to
.BR "{ claude = \(dq\-\-model\(dq }" ,
the only mapping verified against an actual provider CLI; a model
requested for a key absent from this map, or for a
.B spawn\-entry
with no
.BR \(dq\-\-\(dq ,
is a hard spawn error.
.B Merge:
per-key map merge (child wins on collision, parent-only keys survive),
same as
.BR [session\-entry.env] .
.TP
.B liveness\-probe
.B Deprecated
(post-FDR-0017): clown owns the multiplexer attach and names sessions by its
own per-instance key, so this argv can no longer match.
Spinclass now derives liveness from clown's presence index
.RB ( decoration
==
.BR $SPINCLASS_SESSION_ID ),
and this probe is no longer consulted \(em retained only until removed.
Formerly an argv array exec'd after the entrypoint returned, to distinguish
.BR running\-detached
from
.BR inactive ,
inheriting the session env including any keys from
.BR [session\-entry].env ;
e.g.
.B "[\(dqsh\(dq, \(dq-c\(dq,\ \(dqposh -g \\\(dq$SPINCLASS_GROUP\\\(dq list --short | grep -qxF \\\(dq$SPINCLASS_SESSION_ID\\\(dq\(dq]" .
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.TP
.B tombstone\-retention
Duration string (e.g.
.BR 30d ,
.BR 0
to keep forever) controlling how long
.B sc clean
preserves tombstone entries before reaping.
The same retention also governs the cross-session chatroom: messages
older than this window, and stale per-session read cursors, are reaped
by
.B sc clean
(see
.BR chat\-send (), " chat\-read" ()).
.B Merge:
scalar override.
.SS [session\-entry.env]
Subtable map of environment variables injected into every session
process (entrypoint, hooks, liveness probe).
Keys are strings, values are strings.
Values are set
.B literally
\(em unlike
.B [direnv].dotenv
and the
.B [session\-entry].start
argv, they are
.B not
.B $VAR \" / \" $WORKTREE
expanded. For a worktree-relative path, use
.B [direnv].dotenv
(whose values do expand
.BR $WORKTREE )
rather than
.BR [session\-entry.env] .
This is a generic env-injection mechanism with one convention:
.B SPINCLASS_GROUP
is the customary key for a multiplexer-group name so the
.B liveness\-probe
argv can reference it symbolically instead of hardcoding the group in
two places.
Spinclass-owned variables \(em
.BR SPINCLASS_SESSION_ID ,
.BR SPINCLASS_REPO ,
.BR SPINCLASS_BRANCH ,
.BR SPINCLASS_WORKTREE ,
.BR SPINCLASS_DESCRIPTION ,
.BR TMPDIR ,
.BR CLAUDE_CODE_TMPDIR
\(em are applied AFTER this map and so cannot be clobbered.
.B Merge:
per-key map merge (child wins on collision, parent-only keys survive).
.SS [[start\-commands]]
Array of tables declaring custom
.BI sc\ start\- name
subcommands.
See
.BR spinclass\-start\-commands (7)
for the full schema and JSON protocols.
.B Merge:
dedup-by-name (later definition replaces earlier one in-place).
.SS allowed\-mcps
Top-level array of MCP server names to auto-approve in
.IR .claude/settings.local.json .
These servers must be registered externally (e.g.\& in the user's
.IR ~/.claude.json ).
.B Merge:
nil = inherit, empty = clear, non-empty = append.
.SS [[mcps]]
Array of tables declaring MCP servers to register in
.I .mcp.json
and auto-approve.
.TP
.B name
(required) Server name.
Used as the key in
.I .mcp.json
and in
.IR enabledMcpjsonServers .
.TP
.B command
Executable to run.
A name-only entry (empty command) acts as a removal sentinel,
removing an inherited server.
.TP
.B args
Argv passed to
.BR command .
.TP
.B env
Table of environment variables set on the server process.
.PP
.B Merge:
dedup-by-name (full entry replacement, same as
.BR [[start\-commands]] ).
Every entry with a non-empty
.B command
implicitly adds to the allow-list.
.SS [[pre\-merge\-skills]]
Array of tables declaring skill names that the agent must address
before
.B merge\-this\-session
or
.B check\-this\-session
will run the pre-merge hook.
When the resolved list is non-empty, those MCP tools refuse to proceed
until a fresh attestation has been recorded via the companion
.B nothing\-but\-the\-truth
tool, which spinclass registers automatically alongside whichever of
.BR merge\-this\-session " / " check\-this\-session
is active.
The terminal CLI
.RB ( "sc merge" ", " "sc check" )
is unaffected.
.TP
.B name
(required) Skill identifier as it appears in the Claude Code skill
catalog (e.g.\&
.BR eng:code-reviewer ", " simplify ", " security-review ).
A name-only entry (empty or absent
.BR rationale )
acts as a removal sentinel, removing an inherited skill from the
resolved list.
.TP
.B rationale
Free-form string describing why the skill is required for this repo
or sweatfile scope.
The text is echoed back to the agent in the gate-failure response and
must be addressed in the agent's per-skill reasoning.
.PP
.B Merge:
dedup-by-name (full entry replacement, same as
.BR [[mcps]] ).
A non-empty resolved list activates the attestation gate; an empty
or absent list leaves merge behaviour unchanged.
.PP
See
.B PRE-MERGE SKILL ATTESTATION
below for the
.B nothing\-but\-the\-truth
tool's input schema, lifecycle, and gate-failure response shape.
.SS [[remotes]]
Array of tables declaring remote hosts whose spinclass sessions appear
in
.B sc list
and tab completion under a \fIhost\fR\fB:\fR prefix and can be
reattached via
.BI "sc resume " host : id\fR.
Remotes normally live in the global sweatfile.
Completion entries for \fIhost\fR\fB:\fR targets are served from a
per-host cache refreshed by each
.B sc list
\(em completion never networks.
.TP
.B name
(required) The \fIhost\fR\fB:\fR prefix users type.
A name containing
.BR : " or " /
is an
.B sc validate
error.
.TP
.B ssh
ssh destination substituted for the attach template's
.B {ssh}
placeholder.
Defaults to
.B name
when empty, so a name-only entry is a valid all-defaults remote and
.I ~/.ssh/config
does the work.
.TP
.B attach
Argv template exec'd by
.B sc resume
for \fIhost\fR\fB:\fR targets.
.BR {ssh} " and " {id}
are substituted literally in every element; the argv is exec'd
directly (no shell).
Defaults to
.BR "[\(dqssh\(dq, \(dq-t\(dq, \(dq{ssh}\(dq, \(dqspinclass\(dq, \(dqresume\(dq, \(dq{id}\(dq]" .
An empty element is an
.B sc validate
error.
.TP
.B remove
Boolean.
When true, the entry is an explicit removal sentinel that removes an
inherited remote of the same name.
This deliberately diverges from the
.B [[mcps]]
name-only removal sentinel: a name-only
.B [[remotes]]
entry is the most natural declaration of an all-defaults remote (the
ssh destination defaults to the name, the attach template to the
default above), so it cannot double as the removal sentinel \(em
removal must be explicit.
Setting
.B remove = true
alongside
.BR ssh " or " attach
fields is an
.B sc validate
warning (the fields are ignored).
.PP
.B Merge:
dedup-by-name (full entry replacement, same as
.BR [[mcps]] ).
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[[remotes]]
name = "devbox"           # the <host>: prefix users type
ssh  = "sasha@devbox.lan" # optional; defaults to name
# attach defaults to:
# ["ssh", "-t", "{ssh}", "spinclass", "resume", "{id}"]

# name-only = all-defaults remote; ~/.ssh/config does the work
[[remotes]]
name = "lab"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Drop an inherited remote in a child sweatfile:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[[remotes]]
name   = "devbox"
remove = true
.fi
.RE
.SH PRE-MERGE SKILL ATTESTATION
When a sweatfile's resolved
.B [[pre\-merge\-skills]]
list is non-empty, spinclass registers an additional MCP tool,
.BR nothing\-but\-the\-truth ,
and gates
.BR merge\-this\-session " / " check\-this\-session
behind it.
.SS Gate behaviour
On invocation, if no fresh attestation is buffered in the session
state file
.RI ( ~/.local/state/spinclass/sessions/<hash>-state.json ),
the gated tool fails before running any pre-merge hook with a
TAP-14 response containing
.B required_skills
(the full list, each entry's
.B name
and
.BR rationale )
and
.BR required_tool: \ nothing-but-the-truth .
.PP
Each merge attempt consumes one attestation. After consumption the
record is cleared; a subsequent merge requires a new attestation
call against the current diff. There is no sticky once-per-session
mode.
.PP
The terminal CLI
.RB ( "sc merge" ", " "sc check" )
bypasses the gate entirely.
.SS nothing-but-the-truth input schema
.RS 4
.nf
{
  "skills": [
    {
      "name":      "eng:code-reviewer",
      "used":      true,
      "reasoning": "Ran the review; addressed one nil-check finding."
    },
    {
      "name":      "simplify",
      "used":      false,
      "reasoning": "Single-line bugfix, no abstraction surface to prune."
    }
  ]
}
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B skills
(required) Array containing exactly one entry per skill listed in
the resolved
.BR [[pre\-merge\-skills]] .
Names must match exactly; missing or unrecognised names fail
validation.
.TP
.B skills[].name
(required) Skill identifier matching a
.B [[pre\-merge\-skills]]
entry.
.TP
.B skills[].used
(required) Boolean.
True if the skill was invoked for this diff; false otherwise.
.TP
.B skills[].reasoning
(required) Non-empty free-form string. The tool does not police
length, style, or content \(em the act of writing the reasoning
down is the deliverable.
.SS Validation
Validation is strict on presence (every listed skill must have an
entry, names must match exactly, reasoning must be non-empty) and
lenient on content. On failure the tool emits a TAP error listing
missing and unrecognised names so the agent can correct and retry
without re-fetching the skill list.
.SS Mutual exclusivity with [hooks].disable-merge
The companion tool tracks the active gated tool:
.IP \(bu 2
.B disable-merge
unset/false plus a non-empty
.B [[pre\-merge\-skills]]
list registers
.B merge\-this\-session
and
.BR nothing\-but\-the\-truth .
.IP \(bu 2
.B disable-merge = true
plus a non-empty
.B [[pre\-merge\-skills]]
list registers
.B check\-this\-session
and
.BR nothing\-but\-the\-truth .
.IP \(bu 2
An empty or absent
.B [[pre\-merge\-skills]]
list leaves
.B nothing\-but\-the\-truth
unregistered.
.SH MERGE SEMANTICS
Each field uses one of these merge strategies when combining files in
the hierarchy:
.TP
.B "nil = inherit, empty = clear, non-empty = append"
Used by array fields:
.BR claude.allow ", " git.excludes ", " direnv.envrc ", " allowed-mcps .
A nil value (key absent) inherits the parent's value.
An explicitly empty array clears inherited values.
A non-empty array is appended to the parent's list.
.TP
.B "scalar override"
Used by single-value fields:
.BR hooks.create ", " hooks.stop ", " session-entry.start ", etc."
The child's value replaces the parent's entirely.
.TP
.B "dedup-by-name"
Used by array-of-tables:
.BR [[start-commands]] ", " [[mcps]] ", " [[pre-merge-skills]] ", " [[remotes]] .
Entries are matched by
.BR name ;
a later entry replaces the entire earlier entry in-place (not a
field-level merge).
New names are appended.
Removal of an inherited entry is per-table:
.BR [[mcps]] " and " [[pre-merge-skills]]
use a name-only sentinel, while
.B [[remotes]]
requires an explicit
.B remove = true
entry.
.TP
.B "map merge"
Used by
.BR direnv.dotenv " and " mcps.env .
Child keys override parent keys; parent keys not present in the child
are preserved.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/spinclass/sweatfile
Global sweatfile (lowest precedence).
.TP
.I <parent-dir>/sweatfile
Intermediate parent directory sweatfiles.
.TP
.I <repo>/sweatfile
Repository-level sweatfile.
.TP
.I <worktree>/sweatfile
Worktree-level sweatfile (highest precedence).
.TP
.I .claude/settings.local.json
Generated by spinclass from sweatfile config.
Contains
.BR permissions.allow " and " enabledMcpjsonServers .
The
.B spinclass
MCP server itself and its Claude Code hooks
.RB ( PreToolUse ", " Stop ", " PostToolUse )
are loaded once per session via the clown plugin manifest shipped at
.IR <plugin-share>/hooks/hooks.json ,
not mirrored into this file.
.TP
.I .mcp.json
Generated by spinclass when at least one user-declared
.B [[mcps]]
entry is present.
Contains MCP server registrations from those entries; the
.B spinclass
MCP server is loaded via the clown plugin and is not written here.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR spinclass (1),
.BR spinclass\-start\-commands (7),
.BR spinclass\-validate (1)
